4.7 Build a reproducible example
When asking for help, include the smallest data and code that still reproduce the problem:

problem_data &lt;- tibble(
  amount = c("1,200", "not reported", "900")
)

problem_data |&gt;
  mutate(amount_number = readr::parse_number(amount))






A useful help request states:

what you expected;
what happened instead;
the exact error or warning;
a small example; and
the output of sessionInfo() when package versions may matter.

evaluate a source before opening the data;
distinguish raw, processed, and analysis-ready files;
import CSV and Excel files with explicit paths;
inspect dimensions, names, types, and parsing problems; and
write a one-sentence statement of the unit of analysis.

5.2 Newsroom question

What did Norway’s sovereign wealth fund report holding at the latest year-end, and what can this file legitimately tell us?

The analysis begins before R. Open the NBIM all-investments page and record:

publisher and institutional responsibility;
reporting date;
asset type;
available file formats;
definitions or terms of use;
update frequency; and
the URL and date you accessed it.

Authority does not remove the need for scrutiny. Official data can contain classification changes, revisions, omissions, and definitions that differ from ordinary language.

5.6 Read the variables as evidence

gpfg_dictionary &lt;- read_csv(
  "data/processed/gpfg_data_dictionary.csv",
  show_col_types = FALSE
)

gpfg_dictionary |&gt;
  select(variable, description, unit_or_example)






Pay particular attention to units:

market_value_nok is the year-end holding value in Norwegian kroner;
market_value_usd is the same snapshot converted to US dollars;
ownership_pct and voting_pct are stored in percentage points, so 0.65 means 0.65%; and
country is the investment market, while incorporation_country is the issuer’s legal incorporation country.

5.10 Independent task
Choose one row and trace it from the processed CSV back to the official Excel file. Submit:

the company, country, and NOK market value;
the row’s meaning in plain language;
one claim the row supports;
one tempting claim it does not support; and
a screenshot or code output showing the values agree across the two files.

use select(), filter(), arrange(), and mutate();
count rows and distinct entities correctly;
group and summarise with an explicit new grain;
inspect missing and implausible values; and
validate that a transformation preserved the expected total.

6.6 Arrange and inspect extremes

gpfg |&gt;
  arrange(desc(market_value_usd)) |&gt;
  select(company, country, industry, market_value_usd) |&gt;
  slice_head(n = 10)






A ranking is a lead, not a conclusion. Check whether the largest rows are companies, funds, duplicate names, or units expressed differently from the rest.

6.8 Audit missingness before removing it

gpfg |&gt;
  summarise(
    across(
      everything(),
      ~ sum(is.na(.x)),
      .names = "missing_{.col}"
    )
  )






The processed latest-year file is complete. In other datasets, inspect missingness by year, institution, or category before calling drop_na(). Systematic missing data can be the most important finding.

6.11 Calculate a denominator explicitly

grand_total_usd &lt;- sum(country_totals$market_value_usd)

country_shares &lt;- country_totals |&gt;
  mutate(share_of_total = market_value_usd / grand_total_usd) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(share_of_total))

country_shares |&gt;
  select(country, market_value_usd, share_of_total) |&gt;
  slice_head(n = 10)






The result supports a sentence such as: “The United States accounted for X% of the reported market value in the latest equity-holdings file.” It does not show the share of purchases made during that year.

6.13 Checkpoint exercise
Create an industry summary containing:

total NOK market value;
number of holding records;
number of distinct company names;
median holding value; and
share of the overall NOK total.

Then answer:

What does one row in your result represent?
Why might the mean holding value be less useful than the median?
Which check proves the industry totals reconcile with the source?



Show one possible pipeline


industry_summary &lt;- gpfg |&gt;
  summarise(
    market_value_nok = sum(market_value_nok),
    holding_records = n(),
    companies = n_distinct(company),
    median_holding_nok = median(market_value_nok),
    .by = industry
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(
    share_of_total = market_value_nok / sum(market_value_nok)
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(market_value_nok))

stopifnot(isTRUE(all.equal(sum(industry_summary$share_of_total), 1)))

6.14 Independent task
Investigate one region. Produce:

a clearly named filtered object;
a ranked table of its five largest holdings;
a country-level summary with shares within the region;
at least two validation checks; and
a 100-word methods note stating the grain, denominator, and one claim limit.

distinguish stacking rows from joining columns;
combine annual files while preserving the report year and source;
use join keys and relationship checks deliberately;
audit matched and unmatched records; and
pivot between long and wide forms without losing the unit of analysis.

7.7 Pivot to compare columns
Long data are usually easiest for analysis and plotting. Wide data can make a two-year comparison easier to read:

country_change &lt;- country_year |&gt;
  filter(year %in% c(start_year, end_year)) |&gt;
  mutate(period = if_else(year == start_year, "start", "end")) |&gt;
  select(period, country, market_value_nok) |&gt;
  pivot_wider(
    names_from = period,
    values_from = market_value_nok,
    names_prefix = "value_"
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(
    absolute_change_nok = value_end - value_start,
    percent_change = absolute_change_nok / value_start
  )

country_change |&gt;
  arrange(desc(absolute_change_nok)) |&gt;
  slice_head(n = 10)






Inspect missing values after the pivot. A country present in the latest year but absent in the starting year has no valid percent change because the denominator is missing or zero.
Return to long form with:

country_change |&gt;
  select(country, value_start, value_end) |&gt;
  pivot_longer(
    cols = starts_with("value_"),
    names_to = "year",
    names_prefix = "value_",
    values_to = "market_value_nok"
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(year = if_else(year == "start", start_year, end_year))

7.9 Independent task
Choose three countries and create a country-year table for the rolling ten-year period. Then:

confirm there is at most one row per country-year;
pivot it wide and calculate start-to-end change;
identify any missing starting or ending values;
pivot it back to long form; and
write a methods note explaining why the result is about year-end market value, not net investment flows.

move from a broad question to testable measures;
describe a skewed financial distribution with appropriate statistics;
distinguish rankings, shares, and concentration;
inspect outliers in context; and
write a claim that matches the evidence.

8.4 Rank individual holdings

largest_holdings &lt;- gpfg |&gt;
  select(company, country, industry, market_value_nok, ownership_pct) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(market_value_nok)) |&gt;
  slice_head(n = 15)

largest_holdings






Before publishing a ranking, search the source for several top rows and check company names, units, and whether a name describes a fund or another listed vehicle.
Now calculate concentration with an explicit ranking and denominator:

ranked_holdings &lt;- gpfg |&gt;
  arrange(desc(market_value_nok)) |&gt;
  mutate(rank = row_number())

total_nok &lt;- sum(ranked_holdings$market_value_nok)

ranked_holdings |&gt;
  summarise(
    top_10_share = sum(market_value_nok[rank &lt;= 10]) / total_nok,
    top_100_share = sum(market_value_nok[rank &lt;= 100]) / total_nok
  )






Prefer transparent intermediate objects when the result will support a major claim.

8.5 Compare countries with a denominator

country_summary &lt;- gpfg |&gt;
  summarise(
    market_value_nok = sum(market_value_nok),
    companies = n_distinct(company),
    median_holding_nok = median(market_value_nok),
    .by = country
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(share_of_total = market_value_nok / sum(market_value_nok)) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(market_value_nok)) |&gt;
  mutate(cumulative_share = cumsum(share_of_total))

stopifnot(isTRUE(all.equal(sum(country_summary$share_of_total), 1)))

cumsum() uses the current row order, so it must come after sorting. Code can run without an error and still answer the wrong question; order is part of the method.
This deliberate correction illustrates an important point: code can run and still answer the wrong question.

8.6 Compare industries

industry_summary &lt;- gpfg |&gt;
  summarise(
    market_value_nok = sum(market_value_nok),
    holding_records = n(),
    median_ownership_pct = median(ownership_pct),
    .by = industry
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(share_of_total = market_value_nok / sum(market_value_nok)) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(market_value_nok))

industry_summary






Industry share and number of holdings answer different questions. A sector can have many relatively small holdings or fewer very large ones.

8.7 Market value and ownership are different measures

gpfg |&gt;
  summarise(
    pearson = cor(market_value_nok, ownership_pct),
    spearman = cor(market_value_nok, ownership_pct, method = "spearman")
  )






Pearson correlation is sensitive to extreme values. Spearman correlation asks whether higher ranks on one measure tend to accompany higher ranks on the other. Neither correlation proves that ownership causes market value or that the fund “prefers” one company.
Inspect exceptions directly:

gpfg |&gt;
  filter(ownership_pct &gt;= quantile(ownership_pct, 0.99)) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(ownership_pct)) |&gt;
  select(company, country, industry, market_value_nok, ownership_pct) |&gt;
  slice_head(n = 15)

8.9 Independent task
Choose either country or industry as the analytical grain. Produce a short newsroom memo with:

a ranked table;
a share-of-total calculation;
a median and a 90th percentile;
two checks that protect against double-counting; and
a three-sentence finding: one descriptive claim, one comparison, and one explicitly rejected causal claim.

calculate year-to-year and start-to-end changes;
choose a stable comparison set;
distinguish market-value change from purchases and performance;
identify classification and identity breaks; and
report a trend with appropriate caveats.

9.3 Start with annual coverage and totals

annual &lt;- gpfg_panel |&gt;
  summarise(
    holding_records = n(),
    distinct_company_names = n_distinct(company),
    market_value_nok = sum(market_value_nok),
    .by = year
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(year) |&gt;
  mutate(
    annual_change_nok = market_value_nok - lag(market_value_nok),
    annual_percent_change = market_value_nok / lag(market_value_nok) - 1
  )

annual






lag() compares a row with the preceding row, so the table must be ordered by year first. The first annual change is missing because the preceding year is outside the teaching period.
The market value is nominal NOK. A change combines several possible forces:

security prices;
exchange rates;
purchases and sales;
companies entering or leaving the portfolio;
corporate events; and
source classifications or reporting coverage.

The holdings file does not separate these components. Do not describe a rise in market value as “the amount Norway invested during the year.”

9.5 Choose a fixed comparison set
If we select the top countries separately in every year, the membership changes and the lines answer inconsistent questions. Instead, define the comparison set using the latest snapshot and then trace those same countries backward.

top_countries_latest &lt;- gpfg_panel |&gt;
  filter(year == end_year) |&gt;
  summarise(
    market_value_nok = sum(market_value_nok),
    .by = country
  ) |&gt;
  slice_max(market_value_nok, n = 6, with_ties = FALSE) |&gt;
  pull(country)

country_trends &lt;- gpfg_panel |&gt;
  filter(country %in% top_countries_latest) |&gt;
  summarise(
    market_value_nok = sum(market_value_nok),
    holding_records = n(),
    .by = c(year, country)
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(country, year)

country_trends






Check completeness:

country_trends |&gt;
  count(country, name = "years_present")






If a country has fewer than ten years, decide whether the absence is a true zero, a missing record, a renamed category, or a change in investment coverage.

9.6 Compare shares as well as values
Absolute market value can grow while a country’s share falls. Join the annual denominator to the country table:

country_trends &lt;- country_trends |&gt;
  left_join(
    annual |&gt;
      select(year, annual_total_nok = market_value_nok),
    by = "year",
    relationship = "many-to-one"
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(share_of_annual_total = market_value_nok / annual_total_nok)

country_trends






The value trend asks “how many NOK?” The share trend asks “how large a portion of the reported equity portfolio?” A strong story often needs both.

9.7 Audit the industry break

gpfg_panel |&gt;
  filter(year %in% c(2020, 2021)) |&gt;
  distinct(year, industry) |&gt;
  arrange(year, industry)






The source changes from ten industry labels in 2020 to eleven in 2021. Consumer Goods and Consumer Services become Consumer Staples and Consumer Discretionary; Oil & Gas becomes Energy; and Real Estate becomes a separate category.
Do not draw a continuous industry line across this break without either:

building and documenting a concordance to broader categories; or
presenting the pre-2021 and 2021-onward years as separate classification periods.

For a beginner assignment, the second option is easier to defend.

9.8 Audit company-name persistence

company_persistence &lt;- gpfg_panel |&gt;
  distinct(year, company) |&gt;
  count(company, name = "years_present")

company_persistence |&gt;
  count(years_present, name = "company_names") |&gt;
  arrange(years_present)






Only a minority of published names appear in all ten years. That pattern mixes real portfolio entry and exit with renaming and corporate events. A company trend is therefore an advanced verification task, not a simple join on text.

9.10 Independent task
Choose one country from the fixed comparison set and produce a short trend memo. Include:

annual NOK value and share of total;
start-to-end absolute and percentage change;
the largest annual rise and fall;
a check that all ten years are present;
one external reporting question that the holdings data cannot answer; and
a headline that describes the evidence without implying purchases, returns, or causation.

choose a chart based on an analytical question;
build and revise a ggplot2 graphic layer by layer;
use transformations when a distribution is highly skewed;
identify outliers and groups that need further reporting; and
distinguish an exploratory chart from a publication chart.

10.3 The grammar of graphics
Every ggplot2 chart combines:

data;
aesthetic mappings such as x, y, color, or size;
a geometry such as points, bars, or lines;
scales that translate values into visual properties; and
labels and a theme.


ggplot(gpfg, aes(x = market_value_usd)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 40)








The chart is technically valid, but a few very large holdings compress most observations near zero.

10.5 Compare distributions across groups
Boxplots summarize the median, middle half, and more extreme observations. They do not show the total size of an industry.

industry_order &lt;- gpfg |&gt;
  summarise(
    median_value = median(market_value_usd),
    .by = industry
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(median_value) |&gt;
  pull(industry)

gpfg |&gt;
  mutate(industry = factor(industry, levels = industry_order)) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = market_value_usd, y = industry)) +
  geom_boxplot(outlier.alpha = 0.25, fill = "#9ECAE1") +
  scale_x_log10(labels = label_dollar(scale_cut = cut_short_scale())) +
  labs(
    title = "Typical holding values differ across industries",
    x = "Market value in US dollars (log scale)",
    y = NULL,
    caption = "Source: Norges Bank Investment Management"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()








Use the chart to identify industries for closer reporting. Do not infer that a higher median means an industry is safer, more profitable, or preferred.

10.7 Use small multiples for comparison

largest_regions &lt;- gpfg |&gt;
  summarise(total_usd = sum(market_value_usd), .by = region) |&gt;
  slice_max(total_usd, n = 4, with_ties = FALSE) |&gt;
  pull(region)

gpfg |&gt;
  filter(region %in% largest_regions) |&gt;
  ggplot(aes(x = market_value_usd)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 30, fill = "#2C7FB8", color = "white") +
  scale_x_log10(labels = label_dollar(scale_cut = cut_short_scale())) +
  facet_wrap(~ region, scales = "free_y") +
  labs(
    title = "Holding-value distributions vary across the largest regions",
    x = "Market value in US dollars (log scale)",
    y = "Holding records"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()








scales = "free_y" makes the shape within each region easier to see, but bar heights can no longer be compared directly across panels. If absolute counts are the story, use a common y-axis.

design a chart around one defensible takeaway;
order and label comparisons clearly;
show units, dates, sources, and transformations;
remove visual elements that do not serve the evidence; and
write a headline and caption that match the calculation.

11.7 Publication checklist
Before publication, confirm:

the title states one evidence-backed takeaway;
the subtitle defines time, population, and measure;
axes begin at a defensible point and disclose transformations;
categories are sorted meaningfully;
units and denominators are explicit;
labels are readable and not clipped;
color has a semantic purpose;
the caption identifies the source and relevant caveat; and
every number reconciles with a table produced by the analysis.

11.8 Independent task
Take one exploratory chart from the previous chapter and turn it into a publication chart. Submit:

the five-line chart brief;
the final chart;
the table used to verify its values;
alt text of no more than 100 words; and
a short note describing one design choice you rejected and why.

aggregate holding records to a geographic grain;
preserve map geometry during a join;
audit unmatched place names;
choose and disclose a color transformation; and
explain what an investment map does and does not show.

12.4 Harmonise names explicitly
The current source and Natural Earth disagree on three labels. Make the decisions visible instead of hiding them inside a spreadsheet:

country_summary &lt;- country_summary |&gt;
  mutate(
    map_name = recode(
      country,
      "Russia" = "Russian Federation",
      "South Korea" = "Republic of Korea",
      "Türkiye" = "Turkey"
    )
  )

Audit the proposed join:

unmatched_holdings &lt;- country_summary |&gt;
  anti_join(
    world |&gt;
      st_drop_geometry() |&gt;
      distinct(name_long),
    by = c("map_name" = "name_long")
  )

unmatched_holdings






The result should contain no rows. If a later source year produces an unmatched country, stop and investigate it before drawing the map.
Also check for boundary names that have data only under a different definition:

world |&gt;
  st_drop_geometry() |&gt;
  distinct(name_long) |&gt;
  semi_join(country_summary, by = c("name_long" = "map_name")) |&gt;
  nrow()

[1] 60

12.7 Interpret the geography cautiously
This map shows where NBIM classifies the investment market of a holding. It does not necessarily show:

where a company earns revenue;
where its workers or customers are located;
its legal place of incorporation;
the destination of a purchase during the reporting year; or
Norway’s political relationship with a country.

Compare country with incorporation_country to see why a single corporate geography is often inadequate.
Boundary datasets also embed naming and political choices. Record the boundary source and version, preserve the source’s original country label, and explain any harmonisation that affects sensitive places.

12.8 Independent task
Create a map for another year in the teaching panel. Your submission must include:

the country-level aggregation;
an anti_join() table of unmatched source places;
a row-count or match-count assertion;
a ranked table that verifies the five strongest map values; and
a caption explaining the color scale and the meaning of gray areas.

turn exploratory findings into a focused story hypothesis;
build an evidence table before drafting;
separate description, interpretation, and explanation;
verify a financial claim against the source; and
write a transparent methods and limitations note.

13.2 The reporting frame
We now combine the skills from the core workflow. The possible story is not “where Norway sent money in the latest year.” The data do not contain transactions. A defensible frame is:

How concentrated were NBIM’s reported equity holdings at the latest year-end, and how did their nominal market value change over the previous decade?


library(tidyverse)
library(scales)

gpfg_latest &lt;- read_csv(
  "data/processed/gpfg_equities_latest.csv",
  show_col_types = FALSE
)

gpfg_panel &lt;- readRDS(
  "data/processed/gpfg_equities_last_10_years.rds"
)

latest_year &lt;- unique(gpfg_latest$year)
panel_years &lt;- sort(unique(gpfg_panel$year))
start_year &lt;- min(panel_years)
end_year &lt;- max(panel_years)

stopifnot(length(latest_year) == 1, latest_year == end_year)

total_latest_nok &lt;- sum(gpfg_latest$market_value_nok)

country_evidence &lt;- gpfg_latest |&gt;
  summarise(market_value_nok = sum(market_value_nok), .by = country) |&gt;
  mutate(share = market_value_nok / sum(market_value_nok)) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(market_value_nok))

industry_evidence &lt;- gpfg_latest |&gt;
  summarise(market_value_nok = sum(market_value_nok), .by = industry) |&gt;
  mutate(share = market_value_nok / sum(market_value_nok)) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(market_value_nok))

largest_holding &lt;- gpfg_latest |&gt;
  arrange(desc(market_value_nok)) |&gt;
  slice_head(n = 1)

annual_evidence &lt;- gpfg_panel |&gt;
  summarise(market_value_nok = sum(market_value_nok), .by = year) |&gt;
  arrange(year)

decade_change &lt;- annual_evidence$market_value_nok[annual_evidence$year == end_year] /
  annual_evidence$market_value_nok[annual_evidence$year == start_year] - 1

13.5 Separate evidence from explanation
The holdings files can describe:

the year-end value of a named holding;
the number of reported holdings;
country, region, and industry totals;
ownership and voting stakes; and
changes between snapshots.

They cannot by themselves explain why a value changed. Possible explanations require other evidence, such as:

NBIM annual reports and mandate documents;
transaction or flow data;
security-price and exchange-rate changes;
company mergers, spin-offs, and name changes;
interviews with NBIM or independent specialists; and
documentation of classification changes.

A story can report a pattern first and then test competing explanations. It should not promote a plausible explanation to a fact without evidence.

13.6 Verify representative records
Before drafting:

check at least three top-ranked company rows in the official Excel file;
recalculate the main total through a second grouping route;
confirm every percentage denominator;
inspect unmatched categories used in joins or maps;
compare the latest values with NBIM’s own published summary where the definitions align; and
record the source date, download date, and script version.

For example, country and industry totals should reconcile with the company-row total:

stopifnot(isTRUE(all.equal(
  sum(country_evidence$market_value_nok),
  total_latest_nok
)))

stopifnot(isTRUE(all.equal(
  sum(industry_evidence$market_value_nok),
  total_latest_nok
)))

13.9 Independent newsroom assignment
Develop a 600–800 word story pitch based on the holdings data. Submit:

a one-sentence hypothesis;
a five-row evidence table;
one publication chart and its verification table;
two external sources you would interview or consult;
a methods paragraph;
three claims you rejected because the data could not support them; and
a headline that avoids the words received, spent, profit, and return unless you add separate evidence for those concepts.

14.2 Learning objectives
By the end of this chapter, you should be able to:

diagnose a nested unit of analysis;
collapse detailed rows to comparable status cells;
calculate a weighted rate with the correct denominator;
distinguish row frequency from graduate headcount; and
explain why a university ranking may reflect programme composition.

14.7 Calculate a weighted university rate

latest_year &lt;- max(cohorts$year_start)

latest_university &lt;- cohorts |&gt;
  filter(year_start == latest_year) |&gt;
  summarise(
    ft = sum(ft),
    unemployed = sum(unemployed),
    total = sum(total),
    ft_rate = ft / total,
    unemployment_rate = unemployed / total,
    .by = university
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(ft_rate))

latest_university






This calculation weights each study-level cohort by its graduate headcount. The following tempting alternative is not equivalent:

cohorts |&gt;
  filter(year_start == latest_year) |&gt;
  summarise(
    unweighted_mean_ft_rate = mean(ft_rate),
    .by = university
  )






An unweighted mean gives a small cohort the same influence as a large cohort. Use the rate-of-sums for an overall graduate rate unless the research question explicitly concerns the typical programme-level rate.

14.9 A denominator within full-time employment
Suppose the story concerns graduates recorded as authors, journalists, and related writers. The relevant denominator is full-time employed graduates, not all graduates:

journalism_share &lt;- employment |&gt;
  summarise(
    journalists = sum(
      headcount[
        status == "FT employment" &
          occupation == "Authors, Journalists and Related Writers"
      ]
    ),
    all_ft = sum(headcount[status == "FT employment"]),
    .by = c(academic_year, university, level)
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(share_of_ft = journalists / all_ft)

journalism_share






The numerator and denominator now refer to the same status and cohort. A different denominator would answer a different question.

